{"pdfpcFormat":2,"disableMarkdown":false,"pages":[{"idx":0,"label":1,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Abstract: four cryptograms, Z408 fell in a week (Hardens, 1969), Z340 held out 51 years until Oranchak, Blake and Van Eycke, December 2020. Today: how do you know an answer is unique, how do you search for it, and why Z340 was so much harder."},{"idx":1,"label":2,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":2,"label":3,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Keep this brief and sober. Lake Herman Road (Dec 1968), Blue Rock Springs (Jul 1969), Lake Berryessa (Sep 1969), Paul Stine in Presidio Heights (Oct 1969). The letter shown is from June 1970 and contains Z32 at the bottom."},{"idx":3,"label":4,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Z408 was mailed in three parts, one to each paper, with the demand that it be printed on the front page. 408 symbols, 54 distinct ones."},{"idx":4,"label":5,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"The final 18 letters EBEORIETEMETHHPITI have never been convincingly read. The misspellings are in the original. Frequency analysis plus a crib (a guessed word) is the 1969 method."},{"idx":5,"label":6,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Mailed to the Chronicle with a 'dripping pen' greeting card. Many published 'solutions' over the years, none verifiable."},{"idx":6,"label":6,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Mailed to the Chronicle with a 'dripping pen' greeting card. Many published 'solutions' over the years, none verifiable."},{"idx":7,"label":7,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Teaser: these are not unsolved, they are unsolvable. We will prove that with Shannon's unicity distance. Then search, then why Z340 needed both ideas at once."},{"idx":8,"label":8,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":9,"label":9,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":10,"label":10,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"When brute forcing, you first want to know the problem is well posed. Example: much of cryptography rests on Z being a UFD, so factorization is unique, even though finding the primes is hard. Shannon developed exactly the theory for this in the 1949 secrecy paper."},{"idx":11,"label":11,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"0 log 0 = 0. Equality H(X|Y) = H(X) iff independent. Conditional entropy is what we need for equivocation: the uncertainty about the key given the ciphertext."},{"idx":12,"label":11,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"0 log 0 = 0. Equality H(X|Y) = H(X) iff independent. Conditional entropy is what we need for equivocation: the uncertainty about the key given the ciphertext."},{"idx":13,"label":12,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Aperiodicity rules out e.g. a chain bouncing between two states. The convergence theorem is what lets us talk about long-run letter frequencies, and later about where a sampler ends up."},{"idx":14,"label":12,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Aperiodicity rules out e.g. a chain bouncing between two states. The convergence theorem is what lets us talk about long-run letter frequencies, and later about where a sampler ends up."},{"idx":15,"label":13,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Time homogeneous, transition matrix P. English is not exactly Markov but this captures the structure that matters. The entropy rate is the average uncertainty in the next symbol given the current one; for iid symbols it is just H(X)."},{"idx":16,"label":13,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Time homogeneous, transition matrix P. English is not exactly Markov but this captures the structure that matters. The entropy rate is the average uncertainty in the next symbol given the current one; for iid symbols it is just H(X)."},{"idx":17,"label":14,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"A message m and an independent key k are chosen; the codebreaker sees only c = E_k(m). Redundancy measures how far English is from random text."},{"idx":18,"label":14,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"A message m and an independent key k are chosen; the codebreaker sees only c = E_k(m). Redundancy measures how far English is from random text."},{"idx":19,"label":15,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Pairs with P_ij = 0 never occur, so f is finite along the path. The pair process is irreducible on the set of allowed transitions. Ergodic theorem for Markov chains: time averages converge to space averages."},{"idx":20,"label":15,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Pairs with P_ij = 0 never occur, so f is finite along the path. The pair process is irreducible on the set of allowed transitions. Ergodic theorem for Markov chains: time averages converge to space averages."},{"idx":21,"label":16,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Proof: (1) a.s. convergence implies convergence in probability. (2) each typical x has Pr > 2^{-n(H+eps)} and the probabilities sum to at most 1. (3) for n large Pr(A) > 1 - eps and each Pr(x) < 2^{-n(H-eps)}."},{"idx":22,"label":16,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Proof: (1) a.s. convergence implies convergence in probability. (2) each typical x has Pr > 2^{-n(H+eps)} and the probabilities sum to at most 1. (3) for n large Pr(A) > 1 - eps and each Pr(x) < 2^{-n(H-eps)}."},{"idx":23,"label":17,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Heuristic: wrong decryptions behave like uniform random strings, and a uniform string is typical with probability |A|/|S|^n ≈ 2^{-n(log|S| - H)} = 2^{-nD}."},{"idx":24,"label":17,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Heuristic: wrong decryptions behave like uniform random strings, and a uniform string is typical with probability |A|/|S|^n ≈ 2^{-n(log|S| - H)} = 2^{-nD}."},{"idx":25,"label":17,"overlay":2,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Heuristic: wrong decryptions behave like uniform random strings, and a uniform string is typical with probability |A|/|S|^n ≈ 2^{-n(log|S| - H)} = 2^{-nD}."},{"idx":26,"label":18,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"In the notes I bounded 54 log 26 ≤ 54·5 = 270, giving U ≤ 90; either way 408 is several times larger. We are overcounting keys (every map from 54 symbols to 26 letters) and using English redundancy, which is not exactly Zodiac's. Z13 needs only 13 letters and has fewer than U's worth even for a simple substitution."},{"idx":27,"label":19,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":28,"label":20,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"No known structure to exploit, so brute force is out. The chain concentrates on keys whose decodings look like English. Reference: Diaconis, The Markov chain Monte Carlo revolution, which opens with exactly this prison-cipher example."},{"idx":29,"label":21,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Smoothing avoids undefined ratios; logs avoid underflow. The better the language model, the faster we expect convergence."},{"idx":30,"label":22,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"a is a universal constant. Metropolis does not accept every transposition, so this is not our chain: it is the analogous chain with a flat target. The proof is Fourier analysis on S_n: Q is a class function so its Fourier transform is scalar on each irreducible, and the (n-1,1) representation dominates."},{"idx":31,"label":22,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"a is a universal constant. Metropolis does not accept every transposition, so this is not our chain: it is the analogous chain with a flat target. The proof is Fourier analysis on S_n: Q is a class function so its Fourier transform is scalar on each irreducible, and the (n-1,1) representation dominates."},{"idx":32,"label":23,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"tau is any transposition. The transform of U vanishes on every nontrivial irreducible, which is why only the nontrivial terms appear. chi/d at a transposition is the content sum divided by binom(n, 2)."},{"idx":33,"label":23,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"tau is any transposition. The transform of U vanishes on every nontrivial irreducible, which is why only the nontrivial terms appear. chi/d at a transposition is the content sum divided by binom(n, 2)."},{"idx":34,"label":23,"overlay":2,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"tau is any transposition. The transform of U vanishes on every nontrivial irreducible, which is why only the nontrivial terms appear. chi/d at a transposition is the content sum divided by binom(n, 2)."},{"idx":35,"label":24,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Partitions with j near n, and those with negative r_lambda, need separate routine estimates. After k steps the mean of fix is 1 + (n-1) r^k."},{"idx":36,"label":24,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Partitions with j near n, and those with negative r_lambda, need separate routine estimates. After k steps the mean of fix is 1 + (n-1) r^k."},{"idx":37,"label":24,"overlay":2,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Partitions with j near n, and those with negative r_lambda, need separate routine estimates. After k steps the mean of fix is 1 + (n-1) r^k."},{"idx":38,"label":25,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"First row: Metropolis converges to pi, not U. Third row: why the proof breaks; the kernel is no longer a convolution."},{"idx":39,"label":26,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"a, b range over plaintext letters. Strict maximum needs M rich enough to tell f* apart from every other key. Tempering: same proposals, the flat target deformed into one peaked at f*. Simulated annealing (used on Z340) varies beta over time."},{"idx":40,"label":26,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"a, b range over plaintext letters. Strict maximum needs M rich enough to tell f* apart from every other key. Tempering: same proposals, the flat target deformed into one peaked at f*. Simulated annealing (used on Z340) varies beta over time."},{"idx":41,"label":27,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Restarts and annealing are the practical answers to local maxima."},{"idx":42,"label":27,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Restarts and annealing are the practical answers to local maxima."},{"idx":43,"label":28,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":44,"label":29,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"A homophonic cipher spreads each letter's frequency across several symbols to defeat counting. Z408 did this too, but Z340 had fewer occurrences per symbol."},{"idx":45,"label":30,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Unicity says the answer is determined; search found nothing. That contradiction is itself evidence the model of the cipher is wrong. Cruder bound from the notes: 63 · 5 = 315 bits, U ≤ 105; same conclusion. Test ciphers: Berg-Kirkpatrick et al. (2013) argued Z340 is not strictly homophonic because Z340-like test ciphers are solved automatically."},{"idx":46,"label":30,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Unicity says the answer is determined; search found nothing. That contradiction is itself evidence the model of the cipher is wrong. Cruder bound from the notes: 63 · 5 = 315 bits, U ≤ 105; same conclusion. Test ciphers: Berg-Kirkpatrick et al. (2013) argued Z340 is not strictly homophonic because Z340-like test ciphers are solved automatically."},{"idx":47,"label":30,"overlay":2,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Unicity says the answer is determined; search found nothing. That contradiction is itself evidence the model of the cipher is wrong. Cruder bound from the notes: 63 · 5 = 315 bits, U ≤ 105; same conclusion. Test ciphers: Berg-Kirkpatrick et al. (2013) argued Z340 is not strictly homophonic because Z340-like test ciphers are solved automatically."},{"idx":48,"label":31,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":49,"label":31,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false},{"idx":50,"label":32,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"This is the payoff of Part 1: unicity tells you which hypotheses about the cipher are even worth testing. The search over transpositions has to be over a small, guessable family, and the spacing-19 spike says which kind."},{"idx":51,"label":32,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"This is the payoff of Part 1: unicity tells you which hypotheses about the cipher are even worth testing. The search over transpositions has to be over a small, guessable family, and the spacing-19 spike says which kind."},{"idx":52,"label":32,"overlay":2,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"This is the payoff of Part 1: unicity tells you which hypotheses about the cipher are even worth testing. The search over transpositions has to be over a small, guessable family, and the spacing-19 spike says which kind."},{"idx":53,"label":33,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Fig. 24 of the solvers' paper. Nothing in a one-for-one substitution singles out 19, and errors would not create such a spike. Both first suspected columnar transposition, which failed."},{"idx":54,"label":33,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Fig. 24 of the solvers' paper. Nothing in a one-for-one substitution singles out 19, and errors would not create such a spike. Both first suspected columnar transposition, which failed."},{"idx":55,"label":34,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Fig. 26. Z340 at period 1 is only 1.36 standard deviations above the shuffle mean; Z408 at period 1 is 7.52 standard deviations above. Test ciphers: 1,000 artificial homophonic ciphers of length 340 with known English plaintexts."},{"idx":56,"label":34,"overlay":1,"forcedOverlay":true,"hidden":false,"note":"Fig. 26. Z340 at period 1 is only 1.36 standard deviations above the shuffle mean; Z408 at period 1 is 7.52 standard deviations above. Test ciphers: 1,000 artificial homophonic ciphers of length 340 with known English plaintexts."},{"idx":57,"label":35,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Full transposition: Fig. 43 of the solvers' paper (images/transposition.png). Sections 1 and 2 are (1, 2)-decimations. In the last two rows some chunks are reversed. Since gcd(9, 17) = 1 the diagonal walk visits every cell of a 9 × 17 block exactly once. Procedure: start at position 1, write the symbol, move down one (wrapping), right two (wrapping), repeat."},{"idx":58,"label":36,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Fig. 42. Found by Van Eycke. The reading direction runs down-right through the grey line, so one skipped symbol garbles one letter in each of several words."},{"idx":59,"label":37,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"Outer loop: a structured, finite family of transpositions. Inner loop: MCMC over homophonic keys. Unicity says the true transposition, once tried, gives a uniquely determined, high-plausibility plaintext."},{"idx":60,"label":38,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":61,"label":39,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"This closes the loop with Part 1: same argument as for Z408, now applied to the structured transposition family. Compare U ≈ 890 for an arbitrary transposition."},{"idx":62,"label":40,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":63,"label":41,"overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false}]}